      "private": false,
      "reactions": {
        "like": 5
      },
      "restoredAt": null,
      "sourceFeatureExtractionItemID": null,
      "sourceLink": null,
      "spam": false,
      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "I think this really highlights why VRChat’s performance ranking should move beyond just polygon caps and start including mesh VRAM usage in its evaluation.\n\nTake my avatar as an example (tested with Thry’s Avatar VRAM Calculator):\n\nPolygon count: ~257k (way above the 70k “Very Poor” cutoff)\n\nMesh memory size: only 36.44 MiB\n\nTexture memory: ~109.86 MiB\n\nTotal VRAM load: ~146.3 MiB\n\nSo even though the polycount instantly throws it into “Very Poor,” the actual mesh memory usage is still in a reasonable range. In fact, 40 copies of this avatar in a world would take ~5.7 GiB of VRAM — which is heavy, but not catastrophic for most modern GPUs.\n\nCompare that to a 70k poly avatar with a crap ton of blendshapes: the VRAM usage could easily surpass mine because every blendshape multiplies the vertex data stored in memory. That’s why VRAM load is a more accurate measure of performance impact than raw triangles.\n\nA smarter performance ranking system could look like this:\n\nExcellent: < 30 MB mesh memory\n\nGood: < 70 MB\n\nMedium: < 100 MB\n\nPoor: < 130 MB\n\nVery Poor: > 130 MB\n\nThat way, optimized high-poly avatars (with low blendshape overhead) wouldn’t be unfairly punished, while low-poly but blendshape-heavy avatars would be ranked more realistically.\n\nBottom line: Counting polys alone is too blunt of a tool — VRAM mesh size gives a fairer, more performance-accurate metric.",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    },
